  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Defining Architectural Foundations**\n\nI\'m currently focused on structuring the project\'s foundational architecture. Specifically, I\'m working to establish a clear hierarchy, starting with core types and shared models. The initial focus is on defining entities like `UserProfile`, `CandidateProfile`, `WorkExperience`, `Education`, and `Project` within `src/types.ts`. I aim to build a robust foundation that will simplify subsequent development and ensure maintainability.\n\n\n**Expanding Data Structures**\n\nI\'ve been expanding the data structures to include entities like `Job`, `JobSource`, and `JobMatch`, with attributes for scoring and recommendations. I\'m also fleshing out the `Application` and `ApplicationStatus` types to cover the full application lifecycle, from saving to offer or rejection. Furthermore, I\'ve started defining related entities for cover letters, application form responses, Telegram notifications, email events, agent logs, and configuration, and dashboard stats, to establish a functional foundation.\n\n\n\n**Developing Service Interfaces**\n\nI\'m now focusing on service interfaces within the server. I\'m actively defining functions like `parseResume`, `matchJob`, and `generateCoverLetter` to handle key tasks. I\'m also working on `answerApplicationQuestion` and other functions for automating application processes, including email classification. I\'m establishing the structure for multi-source job adapters for Greenhouse, Lever, RemoteOK, and others, including ingestion, deduplication, and pre-filtering functionalities.\n\n\n**Refining Application Workflow**\n\nI\'m currently refining the application workflow logic. I\'ve integrated a waiting-for-approval state allowing users to edit applications before submission. The approval action triggers the submission, transitioning the status to \"APPLIED\".\n\nI\'ve defined `telegramService.ts` for real-time Telegram messages (if configured) and in-app notifications with interactive links. Additionally, `emailService.ts` ingests emails, uses AI to classify and parse data, and updates application status, triggering Telegram notifications based on email content (interview, rejection, offer). The `schedulerService.ts` orchestrates background tasks like job ingestion, AI matching, application preparation, and status checks.\n\n\n**Designing Agent Autonomy**\n\nI\'m now diving into the autonomous agent loop runner within `/services/agentLoop.ts`. I\'m focusing on enabling users to define goals and then having the agent execute tool calls iteratively, with real-time logging of thoughts, actions, and results. I\'m also mapping out the API endpoints in `server/routes/api.ts`, specifically, setting up endpoints for authentication, resume handling, job management, applications, cover letter generation, agent execution, notifications, email processing, scheduling, dashboard stats, and audit logs. I am defining navigation in the frontend, for dashboard high-level KPIs, the application funnel/Kanban board, match stream, AI agent feed, and quick action trigger.\n\n\n**Visualizing Key Functionality**\n\nI\'m now visualizing the core functionalities, focusing on the user\'s workflow. I\'m prioritizing the user experience for job searching and application preparation. The live job listings will feature an instant AI match score badge. I\'m aiming for a straightforward user experience, allowing for one-click \"Prepare Application\" or \"Generate Cover Letter\" triggers.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 10
      nanos: 520809286
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
